WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:04.210
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.210 --> 00:00:11.180
I am a light transport researcher by trade,
and due to popular request, today I am delighted

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:15.580
to show you these beautiful results from a
research paper.

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:20.630
This is a new light simulation technique that
can create an image like this.

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:23.180
This looks almost exactly like reality.

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:27.660
It can also make an image like this.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:34.130
And, have a look at this - this is a virtual
object that glitters.

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:36.510
Oh my goodness.

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:38.090
Absolutely beautiful.

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:39.090
Right?

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:43.289
Well, believe it or not, this third result
is wrong.

00:00:43.289 --> 00:00:48.820
Now, you see, it is not so bad, but there
is a flaw in it somewhere.

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:53.710
By the end of this video, you will know exactly
where and what is wrong.

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:56.059
So, light transport eh?

00:00:56.059 --> 00:00:58.430
How do these techniques work anyway?

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:03.289
We can create such an image by simulating
the path of millions and millions of light

00:01:03.289 --> 00:01:04.550
rays.

00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:10.630
And initially, this image will look noisy,
and as we add more and more rays, this image

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:13.760
will slowly clean up over time.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:18.970
If we don’t have a well-optimized program,
this can take from hours to days to compute

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:20.360
for difficult scenes.

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:25.590
For instance, this difficult scene took us
several weeks to compute.

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:29.430
Okay, so what makes a scene difficult?

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:34.500
Typically, caustics and specular light transport.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:35.780
What does that mean?

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:36.780
Look!

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:42.110
Here we have a caustic pattern that takes
many-many millions, if not billions of light

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:43.880
rays to compute properly.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:49.110
This can get tricky because these are light
paths that we are very unlikely to hit with

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:51.360
randomly generated light rays.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.720
So, how do we solve this problem?

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:59.970
Well, one way of doing it is not trusting
random light rays, but systematically finding

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:03.720
these caustic light paths and computing them.

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:09.670
This fantastic paper does exactly that, so
let’s look at one of those classic closeups

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:13.760
that are the hallmark of any modern light
transport paper.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:15.470
Let’s see.

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:16.470
Yes.

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:21.640
On this scene, you see beautiful caustic patterns
under these glossy metallic objects.

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:26.890
Let’s see what a simple, random algorithm
can do with this with an allowance of two

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:29.880
minutes of rendering time.

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:32.930
Well, do you see any caustics here?

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:35.880
Do you see these bright points?

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:40.750
These are the first signs of the algorithm
finding small point samples of the caustic

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:43.790
pattern, but that’s about it.

00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:49.190
It would take at the very least several days
for this algorithm to compute the entirety

00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:50.780
of it.

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:54.500
This is what the fully rendered reference
image looks like.

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:57.920
This is the one that takes forever to compute.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:02:59.170
Quite different, right?

00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:05.810
So let’s allocate 2 minutes of our time
for the new method and see how well it does.

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:07.860
Which one will it be closer to?

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:09.900
Can it beat the naive algorithm?

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:14.340
Now, hold on to your papers, and let’s see
together.

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:15.400
What?

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:22.710
On this part, it looks almost exactly the
same as the reference.

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:23.900
This is insanity!

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:27.900
A converged caustic region in two minutes!

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:29.340
Whoa.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:32.860
The green closeup is also nearly completely
done.

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:39.610
Now, not everything is sunshine and rainbows,
look, the blue closeup is still a bit behind,

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:43.730
but it still beats the naive algorithm handily.

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:45.590
That is quite something.

00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:51.920
And yes, it can also render these beautiful
underwater caustics as well in as little as

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:53.620
5 minutes.

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:55.320
5 minutes!

00:03:55.320 --> 00:04:00.240
And I would not be surprised if many people
would think this is an actual photograph from

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:01.650
the real world.

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:03.069
Loving it.

00:04:03.069 --> 00:04:08.410
Now, what about the glittery origami scene
from the start of the video?

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:09.590
This one.

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:12.100
Was that footage really wrong?

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:13.620
Yes it was!

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:14.620
Why?

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:16.500
Well, look here!

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:19.269
These glittery patterns are unstable.

00:04:19.269 --> 00:04:22.400
The effect especially pronounced around here.

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:27.970
This arises from the fact that the technique
does not take into consideration the curvature

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:31.330
of this object correctly when computing the
image.

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:37.000
Let’s look at the corrected version, and,
oh my goodness.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:43.190
No unnecessary flickering anywhere to be seen,
just the beautiful glitter slowly changing

00:04:43.190 --> 00:04:45.610
as we rotate the object around.

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:48.050
I could stare at this all day.

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:53.290
Now, note that these kinds of glints are much
more practical than most people would think.

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:59.930
For instance, it also has a really pronounced
effect when rendering a vinyl record and many

00:04:59.930 --> 00:05:01.750
other materials as well.

00:05:01.750 --> 00:05:08.220
So, from now on, we can render photorealistic
images of difficult scenes with caustics and

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:13.630
glitter, not in a matter of days, but in a
matter of minutes.

00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:15.900
What a time to be alive!

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:20.280
And when watching all these beautiful results,
you are thinking that this light transport

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:25.320
thing is pretty cool, and you would like to
learn more about it, I held a Master-level

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:29.210
course on this topic at the Technical University
of Vienna.

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:33.590
Since I was always teaching it to a handful
of motivated students, I thought that the

00:05:33.590 --> 00:05:38.240
teachings shouldn’t only be available for
the privileged few who can afford a college

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:43.210
education, but the teachings should be available
for everyone.

00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:46.150
Free education for everyone, that’s what
I want.

00:05:46.150 --> 00:05:51.530
So, the course is available free of charge
for everyone, no strings attached, so make

00:05:51.530 --> 00:05:55.110
sure to click the link in the video description
to get started.

00:05:55.110 --> 00:06:00.030
We write a full light simulation program from
scratch there, and learn about physics, the

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:56.160
world around us, and more.

00:06:56.160 --> 00:06:59.460
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

